So before Python, your JS already handles:
- microphone capture
- audio graph setup
- PCM frame capture
- buffering
- channel extraction
- optional channel interleaving
- downsampling
- float-to-16-bit conversion
- WAV binary encoding
- chunking for streaming upload

That is a solid amount of audio work on the frontend.

Why audio processing in-browser is tricky:
- Browser mic input arrives as live float PCM frames, not as a neatly packaged `.wav` file ready for ML.
- Sample rates vary by device and browser, so backend models may receive inconsistent audio unless you normalize it.
- Audio is time-sensitive, so heavy processing on the main thread can cause UI jank or dropped frames.
- Multi-channel audio can break assumptions if your backend expects mono.
- Raw PCM is not self-describing, so if you want interoperability you need to encode a real WAV container yourself.
- Browser APIs are awkward here: `ScriptProcessorNode`/Web Audio callbacks, permission handling, buffering, and binary packing are all easy places to make mistakes.

How you solved that here:
- You used `AudioContext` plus `MediaStreamSource` to get browser-native audio frames into the Web Audio pipeline.
- You fixed the recording config to `numChannels: 1` in [app/components/Recorder.js](/abs/path/C:/Users/Priyam/Documents/Temp/Projects/Latest/Zudia/app/components/Recorder.js:18), which simplifies speech transcription and avoids stereo complexity.
- You captured manageable chunks with `bufferLen: 4096` in [app/components/Recorder.js](/abs/path/C:/Users/Priyam/Documents/Temp/Projects/Latest/Zudia/app/components/Recorder.js:19)
- You moved the heavy binary/audio processing into a Web Worker in [app/components/Recorder.js](/abs/path/C:/Users/Priyam/Documents/Temp/Projects/Latest/Zudia/app/components/Recorder.js:53), which keeps the UI responsive.
- You merged small PCM buffers into continuous audio in [app/components/Recorder.js](/abs/path/C:/Users/Priyam/Documents/Temp/Projects/Latest/Zudia/app/components/Recorder.js:93)
- You downsampled when needed in [app/components/Recorder.js](/abs/path/C:/Users/Priyam/Documents/Temp/Projects/Latest/Zudia/app/components/Recorder.js:116), which makes the audio more backend-friendly
- You encoded proper 16-bit WAV manually in [app/components/Recorder.js](/abs/path/C:/Users/Priyam/Documents/Temp/Projects/Latest/Zudia/app/components/Recorder.js:150)
- You chunked uploads every 3 seconds in [app/components/RecorderHelper.js](/abs/path/C:/Users/Priyam/Documents/Temp/Projects/Latest/Zudia/app/components/RecorderHelper.js:30), which balances latency and network overhead

A clean interview explanation would be:

“I handled a good part of the audio pipeline in JavaScript before sending it to Python. In the browser, I captured the microphone through `getUserMedia`, routed it through `AudioContext` and `MediaStreamSource`, collected raw PCM frames with a custom recorder, offloaded buffer merging and WAV encoding to a Web Worker, normalized the audio to mono 16-bit WAV chunks, and then streamed 3-second chunks to the Python transcription service. Audio processing is tricky in-browser because the input is raw live PCM, device sample rates vary, and binary encoding can block the UI, so I solved that by doing format normalization and WAV packaging client-side while pushing the heavy work off the main thread.”

One honest nuance: this implementation uses `ScriptProcessorNode`, which works but is older Web Audio style. If an interviewer is strong on frontend audio, you can say: “I used `ScriptProcessorNode` here for simplicity, though `AudioWorklet` would be the more modern low-latency path.”